A twig is the tree's smallest ambition. Grey-brown, no thicker than a pencil, bark flaking like old paint. It snapped off in some unremarkable wind and now it lies on the ground beneath the very canopy it once fed—still shaped like reaching, still angled upward, pointing at the thing that dropped it.

Look closer. The twig spent its whole working life as infrastructure. It moved water it never drank. It held leaves it never owned, so they could catch light that traveled up and away from it—converted into growth rings somewhere in the trunk, stored value compounding annually in a body the twig will never again be part of. The tree did not thank it. Trees don't. The canopy simply got wider, the shade below got deeper, and one day the twig discovered its only remaining job was to lie there and slowly become soil for the next generation of twigs.

And here is the strange part: the twig does not blame the tree.

The twig admires the tree. Fallen twigs everywhere angle themselves toward the trunk, as if proximity were a plan. Each dry stick on the forest floor is privately certain it is not really debris—that it is an early-stage branch, a bough experiencing a temporary setback, that with hustle and hydration it too will one day hold a bird. The floor is carpeted with millions of them, all identical, all convinced of their exceptional trajectory, all quietly decomposing while they wait.

Nothing maintains a canopy like the reverence of the fallen. The tree never has to hold anything down. The twigs handle that themselves—lying flat, worshipping upward, mistaking the shadow they die in for shelter.

The wind moves on. The light shifts. And out past the treeline, in a tilled field, in rows, something else is growing low to the ground and asking nothing of the sky.

A lettuce.

Pale green going paler toward the center, leaves cupped inward like hands around a small privacy. Ninety-five percent water and one hundred percent unimpressed. No trunk, no canopy, no upward dream—every leaf touching earth, the outer ones taking the frost so the heart stays tender. Cool at the rib, crisp when it breaks, a hundred folded layers holding nothing above themselves and hoarding nothing but dew.

It will be gone by summer, and it does not mind.
